lack. This was the point where Opus 5.5 was most comfortably ahead."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "Opus 5.5's C8 lost 5.0 points, taking the lead from -10.1 to -5.1. The engine preferred F6. It was the first sign of the lead draining away."}, {"turn": 20, "text": "B8 was Opus 5.5's worst move, losing 9.9 points. It took the lead from -8.4 to +1.6, so a White advantage became a Black one. The engine's choice was F6. White's stones by B6 and C8 were tangled with Black's B7 and C7 stones, and this was the moment the game flipped."}, {"turn": 22, "text": "A7 lost another 8.3 points and pushed the lead from +0.5 to +8.8. Two big losses in three White turns turned a comfortable position into a real deficit. By move 32 the board shows two Black stones at B7 and C7 gone and White's capture count at 2, but the score had already moved against White."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "G7 lost 8.7 points and took the lead from +11.1 to +19.7, the biggest Black lead of the game. Opus 5.5 was pushing along the right with G7, H8, H6 and J7 while the engine kept asking for D1 in the bottom left."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "J5 was the 12k's worst move, losing 9.1 points, and it cut the lead from +20.0 to +10.9. Black gave back about as much as White had just lost. Both sides traded 8-to-9-point errors on the right, so Black stayed ahead and Opus 5.5 never caught up."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties with a danger flag, and count.py removes named dead stones and counts area plus komi on the current position. Both only inspect the board as given, with no candidate moves scored and no lookahead.", "stop": false}}